Ladybird draws the text decoration lines of SVG text with the fill and
stroke of the element that declares them, along every stretch of text
below that element. An underline and an overline go under the text,
and a line-through over it. Blink does the same: Its TextPainter::
SvgTextPaintState::TextDecorationObject() finds the element that
declares a line, and that element’s fill and stroke paint it. So does
WebKit, in SVGTextBoxPainter::paintDecoration(). Gecko paints the
lines with the declaring element’s fill only where the text has a plain
opaque fill and no stroke; otherwise its SVGTextDrawPathCallbacks::
PaintDecorationLine() paints them as the decorated text.

The thickness and position of each line follow Blink: a thickness of
a tenth of the font size, an underline half of its thickness below
the baseline, an overline on top of the ascent, and a line-through
centered a third of the ascent above the baseline. Chrome 157.0.8103.79
renders the new test the same as its reference, except for one row
of antialiased pixels on a glyph. WebKit 26.5 draws thinner lines, at
a twentieth of the font size, and takes the metrics of the declaring
element’s font. Firefox 151.0 draws no line for text without a fill.

Blink also skips the ink of the glyphs an underline crosses, and
honors text-decoration-style. Ladybird does neither for SVG text,
and neither does WebKit. None of the three engines paints the lines
in text-decoration-color.

Where textLength adds space between the characters, Ladybird draws
the lines of each character along the character’s own cell. In Firefox
151.0, an underline under such text is in one piece per character, with
none across the added space, and Chrome 157.0.8103.79 and WebKit 26.5
draw nothing there either. Under letter-spacing, the line is continuous
in Chrome and Firefox, as it is in Ladybird.
